WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.220
Background music playing

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:43.000
Reptiles and amphibians are sometimes thought of as primitive, dull and dim-witted.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:53.000
In fact, of course, they can be lethally fast, spectacularly beautiful, surprisingly affectionate and very sophisticated.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:09.000
They have remarkably varied ways of catching their prey and of defending themselves.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:28.000
They can produce a great turn of speed and fight with impressive zest.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:35.000
Some have spectacular colors and show off to one another.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:42.000
They communicate with calls...

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.000
...and with gestures.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:55.000
And there, that's it! The fool works.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:05.000
Reptiles have scaly skins and amphibians soft, moist ones.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:16.000
None of them live at a uniform pace but switch from the fast to the slow lane within a year or an hour.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:23.000
Unlike us, they get their energy directly from the sun.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:36.000
And although being called cold-blooded might suggest they're unemotional, they can be touchingly warm-hearted as mates...

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:48.000
...and as parents.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:56.000
And that's just the beginning. There are a whole lot of other warm-hearted truths to be discovered...

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:12.000
...that give the phrase life in cold blood a completely new meaning.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:25.000
The Galapagos Islands. Some of the reptiles that live here are particularly skillful at solving the problems of getting their energy directly from sunshine.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:32.000
Marine iguanas face a major thermal challenge every morning of their lives.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:42.000
During the night, their bodies cool. And now they must warm up quickly in order that they can become active and start feeding.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:48.000
Their bodies and skins are black, which is very efficient at absorbing heat.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:59.000
And they bask with their black flanks broadside to the sun.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:08.000
The rate at which they absorb warmth is invisible to the naked eye, but very clear indeed to a thermal camera.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:18.000
First thing, they're cold and purplish-blue. But slowly, as they warm up, a golden glow spreads through their bodies.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:25.000
And eventually, after half an hour or so, they become as hot as the rocks beneath them.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:32.000
Once they're thoroughly warmed up, marine iguanas can maintain their body temperature just about as constantly as I can.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:42.000
And what's more, at about the same level, or indeed slightly higher, around 37 degrees centigrade.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:52.000
Now they need to feed. There's nothing to eat on or around these barren rocks except seaweed. And to get that, they'll have to swim.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:05:00.000
But the sea around here is surprisingly cold, around 15 to 16 degrees centigrade.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:13.000
And only the bigger iguanas can absorb enough heat to power the dives to enable them to go to the seaweed at any depth.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:17.000
However, their bodies are now thoroughly warmed up.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:26.000
The thermal camera shows them as golden yellow as they clamber down over the cold blue rocks and dive into the sea.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:53.000
Although their islands lie almost exactly on the equator, the sea here is permanently chilled by a cold current that sweeps up from the depths of the ocean.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:06:00.000
So they won't be able to stay in the water for very long. They have no time to waste.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:13.000
In the shallows, close to the shore, the seaweed has been heavily cropped. To get a good meal, they may have to dive to at least 15 feet, 5 meters.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:23.000
They're able to reduce the chilling effect of the cold water by closing down the blood supply to their limbs and the outer part of their bodies.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:35.000
But even so, their body temperature may drop by 10 degrees or so. A cooling like that would kill a human diver.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:50.000
After 5 to 10 minutes on the sea floor, most iguanas have had enough and they return to the surface and the life-saving warmth of the rocky shore.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:07:01.000
A recently emerged iguana is black. It's chilled to the bone.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:16.000
Now they need heat in order to be able to digest that meal of seaweed. And they get that by spreading themselves on these black, hot, sun-baked rocks.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:29.000
Their image warms from black to purple and then from red to orange.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:41.000
In the evening, the temperature falls and they huddle together to retain their warmth as long as possible.

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:51.000
They will have to wait until the following morning before they can re-warm themselves sufficiently to feed again.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:58.000
Most kinds of lizards have this daily schedule. Side blotch lizards in California certainly do.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:08.000
You can see from the colour of my face that my body is warm. That's because I've got a central heating system which I've fuelled with my breakfast.

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:16.000
In fact, about 80% of what I eat is used in keeping my body temperature high and steady.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:22.000
These lizards, however, squander very little of the energy they get from their food on warming themselves.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:29.000
They, like the marine iguanas, get nearly all they need for that by basking on the warm rocks.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:43.000
And so important is the need for warmth that the females actually choose their males on the basis of which has the best underfloor heating.

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:52.000
Each male sits on his pile of boulders doing press-ups to signal his ownership and to warn off other males.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:09:10.000
Intruders are confronted immediately and, if necessary, attacked.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:20.000
And the victor returns to sit on his wonderfully warm throne.

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:26.000
Look at his rocky kingdom with a thermal camera and it's immediately clear why it's so precious.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:35.000
The rocks are very much hotter than the surrounding grassland and big tall ones catch the sun earlier and retain its heat longer.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:41.000
So not only does the sun warm him from above, his rocks do from beneath.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:47.000
The most powerful dominant male has, naturally, the best pile of rocks.

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:57.000
And not surprisingly, almost all the females.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:03.000
But is it the males themselves or their assets that the females are interested in?

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:23.000
Find out, let's move their hot rocks and give them to a subordinate male.

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:34.000
The females quickly recognize that a more desirable residence has appeared in the neighborhood and start to move across.

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:43.000
And the sex-starved wimp suddenly finds himself amazingly popular.

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:53.000
So the females do indeed go for the males with the hottest rocks.

00:10:53.000 --> 00:11:03.000
These lizards on a small island off the shores of Menorca in the Mediterranean get their heat from another and very unusual source.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:09.000
Ow! Sorry.

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:16.000
They're very curious. I'm the new boy on the block, the new object in their environment.

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:22.000
And that one just gave me a little nip.

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:32.000
They investigate the world around them by tasting it and they're still trying to work out what I am.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:36.000
Their island is rocky and not particularly rich in food.

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:42.000
The lizards are primarily insect eaters, but during the flowering season they also take nectar.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:46.000
They collect it from plants like spurge, which is very common.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:50.000
And they have a very special relationship with this flower.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:54.000
It's called the dead horse arum.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:12:02.000
And it certainly looks like carrion and smells very strongly of carrion.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:08.000
As a consequence of both its looks and its smell, it attracts carrion flies.

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:13.000
And of course, it's the flies that the lizards are after.

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:21.000
But as well as providing food for the lizards, this extraordinary flower helps them in another way.

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:32.000
If this central part, which is called the spadix, is slightly warm, as you can see from a thermal camera.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:37.000
The chemical process that produces the disgusting smell also creates heat

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:42.000
and raises the temperature of the flower by up to five degrees above the surroundings,

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:47.000
sufficiently high for a lizard to warm itself on it on a cold morning.

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:52.000
In case you find that hard to believe, here is confirmation from the thermal camera.

00:12:52.000 --> 00:13:04.000
The purplish-blue lizard quickly takes on the same temperature and color as the arum.

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:11.000
And sitting on arums brings another benefit.

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:13.000
Breakfast.

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:20.000
The fly, lured by the smell, crawls inside.

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:25.000
The lizard hears the fly buzzing within.

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:30.000
The fly, of course, can't find anything it wants, but now it can't get out.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:44.000
The entrance to the flower is blocked by the lizard.

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:55.000
And the lizard gets an easy meal.

00:13:55.000 --> 00:14:02.000
A few months later, the arum flowers have shriveled and produced their fruits.

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:07.000
Until 20 years ago, the lizards ignored these withered bundles.

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:10.000
After all, they hardly looked like food.

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:14.000
But then a particularly inquisitive individual sampled a fruit

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:18.000
and found the soft flesh around the seed rather good.

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:29.000
The habit spread, and now the whole lizard population, uniquely in the Mediterranean, have become arum fruit eaters.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:38.000
They do take a bit of swallowing, but seeds passing through a lizard's gut not only survive, but germinate even more easily.

00:14:38.000 --> 00:15:03.000
As a result, the arums, which were rather scarce here 20 years ago, have suddenly become abundant all over the island.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:12.000
A cold, windswept island off the coast of South Africa is not the first place you'd go to if you were looking for reptiles.

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:17.000
But here on Dasan Island, among penguins and seagulls,

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:24.000
there's one of the greatest concentration of tortoises to be found anywhere on Earth.

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:33.000
There are about 5,000 of them on this one tiny island.

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:39.000
The penguins and other birds, thanks to their warm blood, are active no matter how cold it is.

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:50.000
But the tortoises have to wait for the day to warm up before they can get about their business.

00:15:50.000 --> 00:16:15.000
They bask in the sunshine, powering up their bodies to the optimum working temperature of 33 degrees centigrade, and then they go off to feed.

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:22.000
As the day progresses, the temperature rises quickly, and even before noon it's too hot for comfort.

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:40.000
The tortoises have to head for shade.

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:45.000
In the late afternoon, it gets cooler, and the tortoises venture out again.

00:16:45.000 --> 00:17:01.000
For them, this is the best time. They're thoroughly warmed up, they've digested their morning meal, and they've got energy to spare.

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:16.000
The males begin to fight, jousting like medieval knights, using a projection on the front of the shell like a lance.

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:26.000
The technique is to get the spike under your opponent and then flick him over onto his back.

00:17:26.000 --> 00:17:55.000
Contests can last for half an hour.

00:17:55.000 --> 00:18:23.000
The loser tries to right himself, but the winner keeps biting his legs.

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:31.000
At last, the victor loses interest and goes off to find the female who caused the argument in the first place.

00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:45.000
As for the loser, if he doesn't manage to right himself soon, he may cook in the sun.

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:57.000
Tortoises are able to sunbathe out in the open because their strong bony shell gives them almost complete protection from predators.

00:18:57.000 --> 00:19:07.000
Less well-armoured reptiles, like lizards, are vulnerable, of course, to hawks and coyotes and foxes and cats.

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:17.000
And in the morning, when those warm-blooded animals are already active, the lizards are cold and can't move fast, so they have a problem.

00:19:17.000 --> 00:19:30.000
But they also have a solution. Secret sunbathing. You really can't see them until you're right on top of them.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:42.000
And there's one there. I'm in Arizona, and that, at my feet, is a lizard buried in the sand up to its neck.

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:54.000
Even while it's buried, it can use the sunshine to warm its whole body. It can control the supply of blood to its head so that it pools in a cavity behind the eye.

00:19:54.000 --> 00:20:00.000
Soon, the blood there is as much as five degrees above the temperature of the rest of its body.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:19.000
Then the animal opens the major blood vessels in its neck, and the hot blood circulates so that its whole body is thoroughly warmed, even though it's still mostly below ground.

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:26.000
This is a horned lizard. And very beautiful, too.

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:36.000
This particular species is called the regal horned lizard because it has this splendid crown of spikes at the back of its neck.

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:42.000
When he's hidden, they break up the outline of his head, and so you hardly see him at all.

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:53.000
And now, in the warmth of my hand and in the sunshine, I guess he's warmed up quite a lot. And if I put him down, he now, at last, may be able to run for it.

00:20:53.000 --> 00:21:04.000
And indeed he does.

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:13.000
South African armadillo lizards, which live on these rocky outcrops, have a different solution to the problem of safe sunbathing.

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:19.000
They've turned it into a social activity.

00:21:19.000 --> 00:21:28.000
Whole families of them live together in the crevices among the rocks, and in the morning they all emerge to warm up in the sun.

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:33.000
Of course, there is safety in numbers.

00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:49.000
There are lots of eyes to spot danger, if it appears.

00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:55.000
And when one sunbather takes fright, they all dive for safety.

00:21:55.000 --> 00:22:05.000
If a predator is quick, it is possible to grab one. But even then, an armadillo lizard is not going to be an easy meal.

00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:11.000
They have an additional form of defence. They bite their tails.

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:30.000
And the reason they do that is that it covers up their vulnerable underside and exposes only these very sharp, spiny scales, which is very good protection against predators like snakes or mongooses.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:53.000
They stay like this for quite a long time before they're confident enough to uncurl. I'll put him down and see how he does.

00:22:53.000 --> 00:23:01.000
The sunset necessarily brings an end to activity for most reptiles.

00:23:01.000 --> 00:23:04.000
But not for all.

00:23:04.000 --> 00:23:17.000
A leopard gecko. It, like most geckos, is nocturnal, and it manages to get all the heat it needs from the rocks, which retain something of their warmth for several hours after the sun has set.

00:23:17.000 --> 00:23:20.000
This male is in search of a mate.

00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:27.000
She is less brightly coloured.

00:23:27.000 --> 00:23:34.000
They inspect one another.

00:23:34.000 --> 00:23:47.000
He collects her scent with his tongue and discovers that not only is she female, but she's sexually available.

00:23:47.000 --> 00:23:53.000
He's interested.

00:23:53.000 --> 00:24:07.000
He nibbles her neck and strokes her flanks, all part of his elaborate courtship routine.

00:24:07.000 --> 00:24:12.000
Copulation begins.

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:21.000
This is the time in mammals and birds when the sex of the young is determined, but not in a number of reptiles, including geckos.

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:28.000
Once again, it's temperature that profoundly influences their lives.

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:33.000
The female goes away to lay her eggs.

00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:38.000
She has chosen a place where the temperature is about 31 degrees.

00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:45.000
As her body is the same temperature as her environment, she can't heat her eggs by sitting on them, as warm-blooded birds do.

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:51.000
So they're exactly the same temperature as the rocks beneath.

00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:59.000
After a couple of months, both eggs begin to hatch.

00:24:59.000 --> 00:25:10.000
The first to emerge is a male.

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:13.000
And the second will be two.

00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:16.000
It's the temperature which has determined that.

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:30.000
If it had been a few degrees lower, both eggs would have developed into females.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:36.000
Crocodiles have their sex determined by temperature in a similar way.

00:25:36.000 --> 00:25:54.000
This clutch belongs to the Indian fish-eating crocodile, the gharial.

00:25:54.000 --> 00:26:11.000
The female has heard the calls from below ground made by her hatching young and is helping them to dig their way out of the sand.

00:26:11.000 --> 00:26:18.000
They immediately make their way down to the water.

00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:35.000
And mother goes too.

00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:38.000
Here, of course, they are nice and warm.

00:26:38.000 --> 00:27:07.000
Water retains its daytime heat better and longer than rock, so unlike many other reptiles, gharials and other crocodilians have enough energy to feed actively all night.

00:27:07.000 --> 00:27:25.000
While being nocturnal is unusual among reptiles, it's the norm for amphibians.

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:29.000
Their skin is not scaly and watertight like a reptile's.

00:27:29.000 --> 00:27:32.000
It's soft, moist and permeable.

00:27:32.000 --> 00:27:38.000
If they expose themselves to sunlight for any length of time, they would dry out and die.

00:27:38.000 --> 00:27:46.000
So most frogs only leave their shelters at night.

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:56.000
Since they can't absorb sunshine directly, they either get their heat from their surroundings or draw their energy from the fat reserves that they built up when the feeding was good.

00:27:56.000 --> 00:28:03.000
But even so, they seldom hop unless they have very good reason to do so.

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:10.000
This frog, however, the South American waxy monkey frog is exceptional.

00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:17.000
It's one of the few that can tolerate direct sunshine for any length of time.

00:28:17.000 --> 00:28:36.000
And that is because it secretes a wax from glands on its neck.

00:28:36.000 --> 00:28:48.000
No human sunbather goes to more trouble than they do to make quite sure that every part of their skin is properly anointed.

00:29:06.000 --> 00:29:14.000
The sunshine may also bring them an extra benefit.

00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:28.000
It probably protects them from the fungal infections to which many moist skinned amphibians are prone.

00:29:28.000 --> 00:29:33.000
In the rainforests of Central America, the air is heavy with moisture.

00:29:33.000 --> 00:29:40.000
So the poison arrow frogs can risk basking in the little patches of sunshine that dapple the forest floor.

00:29:40.000 --> 00:29:48.000
And if they begin to dry out, they can retreat into the leaf litter.

00:29:48.000 --> 00:30:00.000
The sunshine gives them sufficient energy to permit the extravagance of calling almost continuously in defense of their territories.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:09.000
They even have enough spare energy to indulge in long battles with their neighbors.

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:58.000
These fights can go on for well over half an hour at a time until both contestants are completely exhausted.

00:30:58.000 --> 00:31:07.000
So a moist skin limits not only where amphibians can live, but how energetic they can be.

00:31:07.000 --> 00:31:13.000
Out in the sunshine, dry skinned reptiles have more options.

00:31:13.000 --> 00:31:23.000
By collecting solar power so efficiently, reptiles need to use very little of the energy they have to survive.

00:31:23.000 --> 00:31:29.000
In fact, they use around a tenth compared with a mammal of a similar size.

00:31:29.000 --> 00:31:32.000
That means they don't have to eat very often.

00:31:32.000 --> 00:31:39.000
A puff adder like this one can wait almost indefinitely for its next meal.

00:31:39.000 --> 00:31:45.000
Amongst predators, patience really is the virtue.

00:31:45.000 --> 00:31:55.000
Whilst waiting for a meal to wander within striking distance, a snake shuts down its body processes so that it uses the minimum amount of energy.

00:31:55.000 --> 00:32:04.000
Only the equivalent of a pilot light is left on, and it can remain like this for weeks.

00:32:04.000 --> 00:32:19.000
All around it, mammals are expending their energy in a way that compared with the snake seems extraordinarily extravagant.

00:32:19.000 --> 00:32:41.000
But when a snake needs to move fast, it can do so with lightning speed.

00:32:41.000 --> 00:32:50.000
Once its prey is secured, a snake can take its time over its meal.

00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:55.000
This gigantic python is feeding on a deer.

00:32:55.000 --> 00:33:06.000
A python kills its prey by wrapping its coils around it and squeezing its victims so tightly and for so long that it can't move.

00:33:06.000 --> 00:33:11.000
But swallowing its meal takes time.

00:33:11.000 --> 00:33:14.000
The deer will go down head first.

00:33:14.000 --> 00:33:26.000
It's much easier that way.

00:33:26.000 --> 00:33:34.000
The ligaments connecting the snake's upper and lower jaw are elastic, and the snake's upper jaw is elastic.

00:33:34.000 --> 00:33:41.000
So that it can engulf the deer's head even though it is much bigger than its own.

00:33:41.000 --> 00:33:48.000
With its mouth stretched tightly around its meal, the snake can't breathe in a normal way.

00:33:48.000 --> 00:34:15.000
But it's able to push the top of its windpipe right out of its mouth and so continue to take in air.

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:32.000
After some hours, all that can be seen of the deer are its hind legs.

00:34:32.000 --> 00:34:41.000
Once the meal has been completely swallowed, the inner workings of the snake's body change greatly.

00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:47.000
Its digestive processes switch to full power and increase their activity 40 times.

00:34:47.000 --> 00:34:52.000
There is an explosion of biochemical activity.

00:34:52.000 --> 00:34:59.000
The liver, the secretions of which power digestion, doubles in size within two days.

00:34:59.000 --> 00:35:06.000
The heart grows by some 40%.

00:35:06.000 --> 00:35:12.000
It will take the python at least a week to completely digest this enormous meal.

00:35:12.000 --> 00:35:20.000
But then it will not need to feed again for months or even a year.

00:35:20.000 --> 00:35:27.000
This ability to switch off helps reptiles and amphibians in another way.

00:35:27.000 --> 00:35:31.000
A baby North American painted turtle.

00:35:31.000 --> 00:35:35.000
It and the rest of its clutch have only just hatched.

00:35:35.000 --> 00:35:40.000
But it's late in the year and the chill of winter has already begun.

00:35:40.000 --> 00:35:45.000
If the hatchlings clambered out of their hole now, they would find nothing to eat.

00:35:45.000 --> 00:35:53.000
So they stay where they are.

00:35:53.000 --> 00:35:59.000
The temperature will fall to minus 10 degrees.

00:35:59.000 --> 00:36:05.000
Ice crystals grow around the babies and even inside their bodies.

00:36:05.000 --> 00:36:11.000
But their tissues are protected by a kind of antifreeze.

00:36:11.000 --> 00:36:18.000
This would kill any mammal or bird.

00:36:18.000 --> 00:36:29.000
They remain in this deep freeze for up to six months.

00:36:29.000 --> 00:36:38.000
But spring comes at last.

00:36:38.000 --> 00:36:51.000
The ice melts around them and eventually within them.

00:36:51.000 --> 00:37:12.000
Slowly they begin to come to life.

00:37:12.000 --> 00:37:32.000
It takes quite a time for them to become fully functional, but eventually they're ready to face the outside world.

00:37:32.000 --> 00:37:49.000
So by allowing their bodies to cool, they have avoided the hard times.

00:37:49.000 --> 00:38:01.000
With the arrival of spring, their parents are now preparing to breed again.

00:38:01.000 --> 00:38:12.000
The male courts the female by gently strumming her cheeks with his long claws.

00:38:12.000 --> 00:38:20.000
And she responds.

00:38:20.000 --> 00:38:24.000
Cold blood is clearly no barrier to affection.

00:38:24.000 --> 00:38:38.000
Reptiles can conduct as complex and as sensitive a courtship as many a mammal.

00:38:38.000 --> 00:38:43.000
This is the biggest of all living reptiles and one of the most feared.

00:38:43.000 --> 00:38:48.000
If one creature were to be labeled a cold-blooded killer, it would be this.

00:38:48.000 --> 00:39:03.000
A saltwater crocodile, a monster that can grow to a length of 20 feet, six meters and weigh a ton.

00:39:03.000 --> 00:39:16.000
But male and female, when they court, blow bubbles at one another.

00:39:16.000 --> 00:39:28.000
She is three times her size and could easily crush her, yet he treats her with great gentleness.

00:39:28.000 --> 00:39:42.000
He strokes her back.

00:39:42.000 --> 00:40:11.000
Slowly, he aligns his body with hers.

00:40:11.000 --> 00:40:26.000
So union is achieved.

00:40:26.000 --> 00:40:42.000
Crocodiles are among the most ancient of reptiles. Their ancestors appeared at about the same time as the dinosaurs. But what about them? Were dinosaurs similarly cold-blooded?

00:40:42.000 --> 00:40:49.000
The rocks of the North American West are particularly rich in dinosaur fossils.

00:40:49.000 --> 00:40:56.000
A hundred million years ago, this was a horizontal mud flat at the edge of the sea.

00:40:56.000 --> 00:41:09.000
And across it came an adult dinosaur with a smaller, younger one trotting alongside, leaving their footprints behind to be fossilized.

00:41:09.000 --> 00:41:17.000
They were iguanodons, a herd of them, together with some bird-footed dinosaurs.

00:41:17.000 --> 00:41:22.000
Were these all solar-powered?

00:41:22.000 --> 00:41:28.000
Some of the ancient reptiles had specific adaptations to help them collect heat.

00:41:28.000 --> 00:41:33.000
This is a plate from the back of a stegosaurus.

00:41:33.000 --> 00:41:43.000
And you can still see the lines where the blood vessels ran, which collected the heat and carried it to the rest of the body.

00:41:43.000 --> 00:41:53.000
So for the stegosaurus, at least, the need to collect heat seems to have been just as important as it is for its relatives alive today.

00:41:53.000 --> 00:42:02.000
But there are clues that suggest that ancient reptiles were better at maintaining their temperature than their modern counterparts.

00:42:02.000 --> 00:42:11.000
This is the jawbone of a very large and very famous dinosaur.

00:42:11.000 --> 00:42:19.000
In life, its head would have been 18 feet, 6 meters above ground.

00:42:19.000 --> 00:42:31.000
This is the jaw of Tyrannosaurus rex.

00:42:31.000 --> 00:42:42.000
An animal as big as this has a very large body mass, which retains heat very well. So perhaps these huge dinosaurs were, in fact, warm all the time,

00:42:42.000 --> 00:42:50.000
simply because they were too big to lose all their heat overnight, as a smaller reptile would.

00:42:50.000 --> 00:42:57.000
But what about when they were small? Were adolescent Tyrannosaurs able to maintain a steady body temperature?

00:42:57.000 --> 00:43:01.000
Were they, in short, warm-blooded?

00:43:01.000 --> 00:43:13.000
Evidence on that can be found in the microscopic structure of their bones.

00:43:13.000 --> 00:43:23.000
This is the leg bone of a young Tyrannosaurus, and it has bands in it.

00:43:23.000 --> 00:43:32.000
The inner section, formed when the animal was young, has an open structure like the bone of a fast-growing warm-blooded mammal.

00:43:32.000 --> 00:43:44.000
The outer part is more dense, more like that of today's reptiles, but whether dinosaurs were really, truly warm-blooded, we may never know.

00:43:44.000 --> 00:43:55.000
What we do know, however, is that dinosaurs were extraordinarily successful and dominated the Earth for 150 million years.

00:43:55.000 --> 00:44:03.000
But there are some reptiles today that can keep their body temperature well above that of their surroundings.

00:44:03.000 --> 00:44:07.000
And these are the tracks of one of them.

00:44:07.000 --> 00:44:15.000
These giants haul themselves out of the sea along beaches like this in many parts of the tropics.

00:44:15.000 --> 00:44:28.000
But in order not to disturb them, I'll turn this light out and we'll look for them with infrared cameras.

00:44:28.000 --> 00:44:30.000
Leatherback turtles.

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:38.000
Like crocodiles, turtles are very ancient creatures, having first appeared at about the same time as the early dinosaurs.

00:44:38.000 --> 00:44:44.000
Today, leatherbacks are the biggest of all reptiles and the most widely distributed,

00:44:44.000 --> 00:44:57.000
for they're found all the way from these warm tropical waters to the freezing seas of the Arctic.

00:44:57.000 --> 00:45:03.000
These have come ashore on a beach in Trinidad where almost certainly they were hatched.

00:45:03.000 --> 00:45:14.000
Now they, in their turn, are laying their eggs here.

00:45:14.000 --> 00:45:28.000
Leatherbacks, we know, can generate heat internally and there is proof of that if you have a look at her eggs that she's laying right now on that thermal camera.

00:45:28.000 --> 00:45:43.000
They are emerging from her body and lo and behold, they are bright yellow, verging on white, proving that they are warmer than their surroundings.

00:45:43.000 --> 00:45:52.000
And she generates that heat within her body from special deposits of fat so that she can maintain her internal body temperature

00:45:52.000 --> 00:45:59.000
up to 8 degrees centigrade above that of the water through which she swims.

00:45:59.000 --> 00:46:17.000
As she sweeps away the surface sand, you can see that the sand too is yellower, warmer than the outside of her shell, for it still retains the heat it acquired during the day.

00:46:17.000 --> 00:46:25.000
So how do leatherbacks retain that precious and expensive internally generated heat?

00:46:25.000 --> 00:46:32.000
Well, to start with, they have their huge size to help them. They really are massive animals.

00:46:32.000 --> 00:46:39.000
This one is getting on to 2 metres, 6 feet long, and they can grow to weigh a tonne and a half.

00:46:39.000 --> 00:46:46.000
And of course, big objects retain their heat very much more readily than small ones do.

00:46:46.000 --> 00:46:56.000
And that is another reason. Now, I am bright yellow going into white, which shows that I'm losing a great deal of my heat.

00:46:56.000 --> 00:47:13.000
But she, on the other hand, is very much darker. And that is because she has an internal layer of fat, an insulating layer just beneath the shell which wraps round her body.

00:47:13.000 --> 00:47:29.000
And the leatherbacks are the only reptiles in the world to have this kind of insulation.

00:47:29.000 --> 00:47:36.000
Her eggs laid, she fills in the hole with sand.

00:47:36.000 --> 00:47:40.000
And now she's on her way back to the sea.

00:47:40.000 --> 00:47:49.000
Life in cold blood has been a great success. It has, after all, endured for some 350 million years.

00:47:49.000 --> 00:47:57.000
But how did it all begin? To find the answer to that, we have to go back in time and back to the water,

00:47:57.000 --> 00:48:02.000
to the age when strange fish were hauling themselves up onto the land.

00:48:02.000 --> 00:48:20.000
Fish that were the ancestors of the amphibians.

00:48:20.000 --> 00:48:25.000
Amphibians and reptiles are not easy creatures to film.

00:48:25.000 --> 00:48:34.000
They certainly do interesting things, but they also spend a great deal of time doing nothing much.

00:48:34.000 --> 00:48:41.000
We needed the help of scientists who really understood these creatures.

00:48:41.000 --> 00:48:47.000
Some workers have spent over 20 years studying their animals, both in the lab and in the field.

00:48:47.000 --> 00:48:53.000
They investigate the lives of their chosen species using all kinds of gear.

00:48:53.000 --> 00:49:07.000
Some sophisticated, some perhaps less so.

00:49:07.000 --> 00:49:21.000
With their help, we had a rare chance to get under the skin of some of our subjects.

00:49:21.000 --> 00:49:27.000
Madagascar was going to be a very important location for us.

00:49:27.000 --> 00:49:39.000
It's a huge island, a thousand miles long, with a great variety of habitats, and it's extraordinarily rich in reptiles.

00:49:39.000 --> 00:49:50.000
I first went to Madagascar back in 1960, filming for a series called Zoo Quest.

00:49:50.000 --> 00:49:56.000
I was trying to film all kinds of creatures, including the monkey-like lemurs and many rare birds.

00:49:56.000 --> 00:50:04.000
But I was particularly fascinated by the island's chameleons.

00:50:04.000 --> 00:50:15.000
There are, in fact, more species of chameleons in Madagascar than in all the rest of the world put together.

00:50:15.000 --> 00:50:21.000
There is one, the pygmy leaf chameleon, which was said to be only an inch or so long.

00:50:21.000 --> 00:50:26.000
I yearned to see it, but I never found it.

00:50:26.000 --> 00:50:35.000
Now I was back, and this time reptiles were our sole subject.

00:50:35.000 --> 00:50:41.000
Although Madagascar is only separated from the east coast of Africa by 300 miles of sea,

00:50:41.000 --> 00:50:47.000
its people, and particularly its animals, are very different indeed from those on the continent,

00:50:47.000 --> 00:50:52.000
with hundreds of species that are found nowhere else in the world.

00:50:52.000 --> 00:50:58.000
Once again, I was in search of chameleons.

00:50:58.000 --> 00:51:25.000
In the old days, television, all television was black and white. But now I could film and record chameleons in color, and what colors they have.

00:51:25.000 --> 00:51:31.000
It had come in the rainy season, when most creatures, including reptiles, tend to breed,

00:51:31.000 --> 00:51:35.000
and are therefore particularly active and interesting.

00:51:35.000 --> 00:51:44.000
And this time I had the help of Bertrand Raza-Hammatra, a Malagasy naturalist who's made a particular study of chameleons.

00:51:44.000 --> 00:51:49.000
He's worked on them for over 10 years and knows most kinds very well.

00:51:49.000 --> 00:51:54.000
I asked him about the pygmy species that had fascinated me for so long.

00:51:54.000 --> 00:51:56.000
I mean, that really is full grown?

00:51:56.000 --> 00:51:58.000
Yes, full grown.

00:51:58.000 --> 00:52:00.000
But then it's only that big?

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:05.000
Yeah, it's very small.

00:52:05.000 --> 00:52:12.000
He suggested that although chameleons are mostly active during the day, we should look for them at night,

00:52:12.000 --> 00:52:22.000
because most of them turn pale in the dark and are therefore easily picked out in the light of our torches.

00:52:22.000 --> 00:52:28.000
Ah!

00:52:28.000 --> 00:52:30.000
What is that?

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:31.000
This is...

00:52:31.000 --> 00:52:32.000
What species?

00:52:32.000 --> 00:52:34.000
This is Oosteletts.

00:52:34.000 --> 00:52:36.000
Oosteletts, aye, aye.

00:52:36.000 --> 00:52:38.000
And male or female?

00:52:38.000 --> 00:52:39.000
Female.

00:52:39.000 --> 00:52:40.000
How do you know?

00:52:40.000 --> 00:52:42.000
The color.

00:52:42.000 --> 00:52:43.000
There's another one?

00:52:43.000 --> 00:52:45.000
Oh, there's another.

00:52:45.000 --> 00:52:50.000
This one was far from upset at being woken up.

00:52:50.000 --> 00:52:53.000
Ah!

00:52:53.000 --> 00:52:56.000
It's fed!

00:52:56.000 --> 00:52:58.000
That's absolutely extraordinary.

00:52:58.000 --> 00:53:03.000
It can't possibly feed normally in the darkness.

00:53:03.000 --> 00:53:06.000
It just takes advantage of our light and finds an insect.

00:53:06.000 --> 00:53:08.000
Bravo.

00:53:08.000 --> 00:53:12.000
Let's go and see if we can find more.

00:53:12.000 --> 00:53:18.000
Bertrand explained that there was another reason why night was the best time to look for chameleons.

00:53:18.000 --> 00:53:22.000
When they go to sleep, they climb to the very far end of branches,

00:53:22.000 --> 00:53:25.000
where they're out of the way of predators such as snakes.

00:53:25.000 --> 00:53:27.000
There's another.

00:53:27.000 --> 00:53:43.000
And of course, that was where we found them, just as he said.

00:53:43.000 --> 00:53:48.000
Oh, that's a big one.

00:53:48.000 --> 00:53:51.000
Beautiful.

00:53:51.000 --> 00:53:54.000
Yes.

00:53:54.000 --> 00:53:59.000
Well, this one is just a baby.

00:53:59.000 --> 00:54:01.000
And how old do you think that is?

00:54:01.000 --> 00:54:03.000
I think just a few days.

00:54:03.000 --> 00:54:07.000
A few days?

00:54:07.000 --> 00:54:12.000
So even when it's nearly hatched, it knows to come to the end of the branch.

00:54:12.000 --> 00:54:14.000
Yes.

00:54:14.000 --> 00:54:18.000
Look, they chose the tip of a branch.

00:54:18.000 --> 00:54:20.000
Yes.

00:54:20.000 --> 00:54:22.000
Very difficult to get.

00:54:22.000 --> 00:54:25.000
Of course, if it was in the day, a bird could get it.

00:54:25.000 --> 00:54:26.000
But at night, safe.

00:54:26.000 --> 00:54:29.000
At night, safe.

00:54:29.000 --> 00:54:44.000
In 1960, my chameleon hunting techniques weren't quite so expert.

00:54:44.000 --> 00:55:00.000
However, I did discover that if you put a stick in front of a chameleon,

00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:02.000
it will usually obligingly walk onto it.

00:55:02.000 --> 00:55:04.000
But now, with Bertrand as my guide,

00:55:04.000 --> 00:55:08.000
we could search for the wonderful species that I had failed to find before.

00:55:08.000 --> 00:55:12.000
They'd be down here.

00:55:12.000 --> 00:55:19.000
The chameleon was on the ground, almost invisible among the leaf litter.

00:55:19.000 --> 00:55:21.000
That?

00:55:21.000 --> 00:55:29.000
But Bertrand spotted it.

00:55:29.000 --> 00:55:35.000
How extraordinary.

00:55:35.000 --> 00:55:39.000
This is a pygmy leaf chameleon.

00:55:39.000 --> 00:55:42.000
This is the smallest chameleon in the world.

00:55:42.000 --> 00:55:44.000
In the world?

00:55:44.000 --> 00:55:47.000
And probably the smallest reptiles in the world.

00:55:47.000 --> 00:55:50.000
Of any kind.

00:55:50.000 --> 00:56:02.000
You know, I'd heard about these, and I was here in Madagascar 47 years ago.

00:56:02.000 --> 00:56:08.000
And I read about these, and I never saw one.

00:56:08.000 --> 00:56:15.000
I think it was because I never knew they were as small as this.

00:56:15.000 --> 00:56:18.000
That is absolutely extraordinary.

00:56:18.000 --> 00:56:22.000
It's about the size of a blue bottle.

00:56:22.000 --> 00:56:25.000
A blowfly.

00:56:25.000 --> 00:56:28.000
And what does it feed on?

00:56:28.000 --> 00:56:30.000
A small fly.

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:31.000
Small flies?

00:56:31.000 --> 00:56:33.000
Yeah.

00:56:33.000 --> 00:56:37.000
Absolutely wonderful.

00:56:37.000 --> 00:56:40.000
I am astonished.

00:56:40.000 --> 00:56:48.000
That is the most marvelous thing I've seen for a very, very long time.

00:56:48.000 --> 00:56:54.000
Finding the pygmy chameleon would not have been possible without Bertrand's expertise and sharp eyes.

00:56:54.000 --> 00:57:09.000
He's just one of the scientists who has helped to reveal to us the secret lives of reptiles and amphibians.

00:57:09.000 --> 00:57:25.000
To find out more about Open University programs, call 0870 787 4455 or go to bbc.co.uk slash nature.

00:57:25.000 --> 00:57:40.000
Next week, join me for the story of the amorous amphibians, the land invaders.

00:57:55.000 --> 00:57:57.000
Thank you.
